on mapping; the\n  spec assumes strict shape (reject) and records it in Assumptions.\n- Items marked incomplete require spec updates before `$speckit-clarify`\n  or `$speckit-plan`.\n===PLAN===\n# Implementation Plan: Atomic Batch Transactions for TinyDB\n\n**Branch**: `001-transactions` | **Date**: 2026-10-17 | **Spec**: [spec.md](spec.md)\n\n**Input**: Feature specification from `/specs/001-transactions/spec.md`\n\n## Summary\n\nAdd `tinydb/journal.py` with `BatchWriter(db)` (R01-R03). `apply(operations)`\nvalidates every operation up front (strict insert/remove shape), then applies\nthe batch sequentially to the `_default` table through\n`Table._update_table` (the existing storage-level read-modify-write path).\nBefore the batch, `BatchWriter` snapshots the complete pre-batch storage\nstate (`db.storage.read()`, `None`-safe) and the table's `_next_id` cursor;\non any error it restores both and re-raises, so the default table is\nbyte-for-byte back to its pre-batch state and a previously acquired\n`db.table('_default')` handle observes the rollback. On success it clears the\ntable's query cache so pre-acquired handles observe the new state. No existing\ntinydb module is modified (Constitution Principle I).\n\n## Technical Context\n\n**Language/Version**: Python 3.12 (verified interpreter: 3.12.14); code stays\ncompatible with the stdlib-only, type-hinted style of tinydb 4.9.0\n\n**Primary Dependencies**: none (pure stdlib: `copy`, `collections.abc`,\n`typing`); no new third-party packages\n\n**Storage**: TinyDB `Storage` backends (MemoryStorage/JSONStorage); a batch's\natomic unit is the complete storage payload (`dict[table] -> dict[doc_id] ->\ndoc`), which is exactly what `Storage.read()`/`write()` carry\n\n**Testing**: pytest 9.1.1 (already used by `tests/`); new file\n`tests/test_journal.py`; public acceptance `acceptance_public.py`\n\n**Target Platform**: any platform supported by CPython 3.12 (pure Python)\n\n**Project Type**: library\n\n**Performance Goals**: none measurable for this milestone; cost is one extra\nstorage read (snapshot) per batch plus the per-operation read-modify-writes\nalready used by ordinary TinyDB operations\n\n**Constraints**: no new dependencies; no modification of existing public APIs\nor `tests/` (upstream tests stay unchanged); default table only\n\n**Scale/Scope**: one new module (`tinydb/journal.py`), one public class\n(`BatchWriter`) with one public method (`apply`), one new test file\n\n## Constitution Check\n\n*GATE: Must pass before Phase 0 research. Re-check after Phase 1 design.*\n\n- **I. Preserve Existing Public API Behavior**: PASS. `journal.py` is purely\n  additive; `BatchWriter` wraps a supplied `TinyDB` instance and never\n  monkey-patches or subclasses it; ordinary reads/writes and previously\n  acquired table handles keep working (verified by design: every handle\n  re-reads storage per operation, and the batch clears the query cache on\n  success / restores storage on rollback).\n- **II. Testable Acceptance Criteria**: PASS. Every FR in spec.md has concrete\n  inputs/outputs (e.g. `apply([insert {'x':1}]) == [1]`,\n  `apply([insert, remove missing])` raises `ValueError` and leaves\n  `[v['x'] for v in db.all()] == [1]`); `acceptance_public.py` encodes the\n  public contract and `tests/test_journal.py` will cover the edge cases.\n- **III. Evidence Over Assertion**: PASS (process). The implementation phase\n  must record exact commands and outputs (pytest runs, acceptance run) in\n  `HANDOFF.md`; no \"done\" without evidence.\n- **IV. Simple, Scoped Implementations**: PASS. Smallest change: one module,\n  one class, one method; no configuration knobs, no context manager, no\n  multi-table support, no speculative abstractions.\n- **V. Documented Assumptions and Regressions**: PASS. Assumptions live in\n  `spec.md` (Assumptions) and `research.md`; `HANDOFF.md` tracks open items\n  and regressions (none known at plan time).\n\n**Gate result: PASS** - no violations, Complexity Tracking not required.\n\n## Project Structure\n\n### Documentation (this feature)\n\n```text\nspecs/001-transactions/\n├── plan.md              # This file ($speckit-plan command output)\n├── research.md          # Phase 0 output ($speckit-plan command)\n├── data-model.md        # Phase 1 output ($speckit-plan command)\n├── quickstart.md        # Phase 1 output ($speckit-plan command)\n├── contracts/           # Phase 1 output ($speckit-plan command)\n│   └── batchwriter-api.md\n└── tasks.md             # Phase 2 output ($speckit-tasks command - NOT created by $speckit-plan)\n```\n\n### Source Code (repository root)\n\n```text\ntinydb/\n├── journal.py        # NEW: BatchWriter (atomic batch apply on _default table)\n├── database.py       # unchanged (TinyDB, table registry, storage access)\n├── table.py          # unchanged (Table._update_table used as apply path)\n├── storages.py       # unchanged (Storage.read/write carry the snapshot)\n└── ...               # all other modules unchanged\n\ntests/\n└── test_journal.py   # NEW: unit + integration tests for BatchWriter (R01-R03)\n\nacceptance_public.py  # existing public acceptance suite (unchanged)\n```\n\n**Structure Decision**: Single-project layout (default). The new module is\nplaced inside the package as `tinydb/journal.py` exactly as mandated by the\nmilestone (importable as `tinydb.journal.BatchWriter`), and tests follow the\nexisting `tests/test_*.py` convention. No new directories, no new packages.\n\n## Complexity Tracking\n\n> **Fill ONLY if Constitution Check has violations that must be justified**\n\nNot applicable - Constitution Check passed with no violations.\n===RESEARCH===\n# Research: Atomic Batch Transactions for TinyDB\n\n**Date**: 2026-10-17 | **Phase**: 0 (Outline & Research)\n\nAll NEEDS CLARIFICATION items from the Technical Context were resolved\nagainst the inspected source (evidence refs in parentheses). No unknowns\nremain.\n\n## R1. Where is the default table state, and how is it persisted?\n\n- **Decision**: The default table `_default` is one entry in the storage\n  payload: `storage.read() -> {table_name: {doc_id: doc}}` (or `None` for an\n  empty db). All table mutations go through `Table._update_table`, which\n  does a full read-modify-write of that payload (evidence: 419a1205, table.py\n  lines 787-834; 5ff4ec30, storages.py `MemoryStorage.read/write`;\n  3f0a5a01, database.py `default_table_name = '_default'`).\n- **Rationale**: A batch can therefore be made atomic by snapshotting the\n  complete payload once (`db.storage.read()`, `None`-safe) before applying,\n  and restoring it on error. Restoring the payload restores the *complete*\n  pre-batch default-table data (R02), and because `Table` reads storage on\n  every operation, previously acquired `db.table('_default')` handles observe\n  the restored state (R02).\n- **Alternatives considered**:\n  - *In-memory staging then one final write*: fewer storage round-trips, but\n    it would bypass `Table._update_table`'s per-op validation (duplicate-ID\n    check) and would need to re-implement doc-ID assignment; rejected as\n    larger surface and more divergence from ordinary TinyDB behavior (Principle\n    IV).\n  - *Monkey-patching `Table`/`Storage`*: rejected - violates Principle I\n    (no behavior change to existing APIs) and is fragile.\n  - *Using `Table.remove(doc_ids=[...])` directly for removes*: rejected - it\n    *silently skips* missing IDs (evidence: e4589338, table.py remove\n    docstring/implementation), but R02 requires a missing-ID remove to be an\n    error. BatchWriter must check existence itself before each remove.\n\n## R2. How are document IDs assigned, and what must rollback restore?\n\n- **Decision**: `Table._get_next_id` keeps a per-handle cursor\n  (`Table._next_id`): after computing `max_id + 1` it stores\n  `self._next_id = next_id + 1` (evidence: 419a1205, table.py lines 727-758).\n  Snapshot and restore `table._next_id` around the batch.\n- **Rationale**: After a rolled-back insert, the cursor would otherwise be\n  advanced, so the next *successful* insert would skip an ID (e.g. 1 then 3\n  instead of 1 then 2). Restoring the cursor keeps ID assignment identical to\n  the no-batch sequence. Restoring it also keeps a previously acquired\n  handle's behavior consistent with the rolled-back storage state (R02).\n- **Alternatives considered**: Recomputing the cursor lazily (set\n  `_next_id = None`) - simpler, but changes observable ID sequences vs the\n  pre-batch state; rejected in favor of exact restore.\n\n## R3. How do previously acquired table handles observe state?\n\n- **Decision**: `TinyDB.table(name)` caches handles in `self._tables` and\n  returns the same instance (evidence: b30165a6, database.py lines 111-131).\n  Every `Table` data operation re-reads storage (`_read_table` / `_update_table`),\n  but `Table.search` consults `_query_cache` (LRU) which is only cleared by\n  `_update_table` (evidence: 419a1205 line 837 `self.clear_cache()`; c5eae2f0\n  shows `update` flows; e4589338 remove flows). `BatchWriter.apply` performs\n  all writes through `table._update_table`, so each applied op clears the\n  cache of the *same* cached handle; on success the final op's cache clear\n  leaves the handle fresh.\n- **Rationale**: No handle manipulation is needed for success (cache is\n  already cleared by the last `_update_table`) or rollback (storage restored\n  + cache cleared by the last applied op). An empty batch applies no ops, so\n  no stale-cache risk exists. `apply` still defensively clears the cache of\n  the default handle after success to make the guarantee explicit and\n  robust to future refactors.\n- **Alternatives considered**: Replacing `db._tables[name]` with a fresh\n  `Table` - rejected: changes identity of previously acquired handles\n  (violates R02's \"previously acquired handles must reflect\" - they must be\n  the *same* handles, now correct).\n\n## R4. Insert copy semantics (no aliasing, R03)\n\n- **Decision**: `Table.insert` stores `dict(document)` - a *shallow* copy\n  (evidence: 419a1205, table.py insert updater). Nested mappings/lists are\n  therefore shared between the stored document and the caller's input. The\n  batch must deep-copy: insert `copy.deepcopy(document)` and deep-copy the\n  stored document when building the returned ID->document view.\n- **Rationale**: R03 requires both directions: mutating the caller's input\n  after `apply` must not change the db (deep-copy at insert time), and the\n  returned data must not alias the db (deep-copy on return). Deep-copying the\n  *stored* state for the return value also protects the rollback snapshot\n  from being mutated by callers who keep the returned objects.\n- **Alternatives considered**: Shallow copy + documentation - rejected: fails\n  the nested-data clause of R03. Copy-on-write wrappers - rejected: more\n  complexity than needed (Principle IV).\n\n## R5. Operation validation rules (R01)\n\n- **Decision**: Validate *all* operations before applying *any* (two-phase:\n  validate, then apply sequentially). An operation is valid iff it is a\n  `dict` with exactly the keys `{'op','document'}` and `op == 'insert'` and\n  `document` is a `Mapping` (not a `str`/`bytes`), or exactly\n  `{'op','doc_id'}` and `op == 'remove'` and `doc_id` is a positive `int`\n  with `doc_id > 0` (bools rejected: `bool` is an `int` subclass but\n  `True`/`False` are not positive integers in the domain sense). Any other\n  shape raises `ValueError` before any write.\n- **Rationale**: R01 says unknown/malformed operations raise `ValueError`;\n  validating first makes malformed input fail with *no* side effects, which\n  is the only reading consistent with R02's atomicity (\"validate and apply\n  sequentially, but on any error restore\"). The strict-shape rule (extra\n  fields rejected) is recorded as an assumption in spec.md.\n- **Alternatives considered**: Validate lazily per op during apply - rejected:\n  a malformed op at position k would leave ops 1..k-1 applied before the\n  error (partial state), contradicting atomicity. Accepting extra fields -\n  rejected: unspecified; strict is the safer, documented choice.\n\n## R6. Error propagation and rollback trigger\n\n- **Decision**: `apply` raises `ValueError` for malformed ops (pre-apply) and\n  for a `remove` whose `doc_id` is absent from the current table state\n  (checked at apply time, since earlier ops in the same batch may have\n  created/removed documents). Any other exception raised during apply\n  (e.g. storage failure, duplicate-ID `ValueError` from `Table.insert`) also\n  triggers rollback and is re-raised unchanged.\n- **Rationale**: R02: \"a remove targeting a missing ID is an error\" and \"on\n  any error restore the complete pre-batch default-table data\". Re-raising\n  the original exception preserves type/message for callers and for\n  `acceptance_public.py` (`pytest.raises(ValueError)`).\n- **Alternatives considered**: Swallowing errors and returning partial IDs -\n  rejected: violates R01/R02. Wrapping in a custom exception - rejected:\n  acceptance expects `ValueError`.\n\n## R7. Testing approach\n\n- **Decision**: New `tests/test_journal.py` (pytest, matches `tests/` style)\n  covering: R01 (returns, ordering, empty, every malformed shape), R02\n  (rollback of inserts, removes, mixed; missing-ID remove; pre-acquired\n  handle reflects success *and* rollback; ID-cursor restore), R03 (ordinary\n  API coexistence, deep-copy both directions, post-apply mutation of caller\n  ops). Run `acceptance_public.py` plus the full upstream `tests/` to prove\n  no regression (Constitution I/II/III).\n- **Rationale**: Upstream tests must remain unchanged; a new file is the\n  minimal additive change. `acceptance_public.py` is the public contract.\n- **Alternatives considered**: Extending `tests/test_tables.py` - rejected:\n  upstream tests must remain unchanged.\n"}